Expert: Great observation! async/await makes asynchronous code much easier to write and read. HTTP 
requests are asynchronous operations - they take time to complete and we don't want to block the rest of 
our code while waiting.
The async keyword marks a function as asynchronous, meaning it will return a Promise. The await 
keyword can only be used inside an async function and makes JavaScript wait until the Promise resolves 
before continuing.
Without async/await, we'd have to use Promise chains with .then() and .catch(), which can become 
hard to read:
function getAllProducts() {
  return fetch('http://localhost:3000/products')
    .then(response => {
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
      }
      return response.json();
    })
    .then(products => {
      console.log('All products:', products);
      return products;
    })
    .catch(error => {
      console.error('Error fetching products:', error);
    });
}
